IPv4 Addressing

The lab uses private IPv4 addressing.

A typical small-business network can use a structure such as:

Network:       192.168.x.0/24
Gateway:       192.168.x.1
DNS Server:    192.168.x.x
DHCP Range:    192.168.x.100 - 192.168.x.200

Example addresses are provided for documentation purposes only. They are not the actual network configuration.

Static IP Configuration

Servers and infrastructure devices should normally use predictable IP addresses.

A static configuration includes:

IPv4 address
Subnet mask
Default gateway
Preferred DNS server
Alternate DNS server where appropriate

Windows PowerShell can be used to inspect network configuration:

ipconfig /all

Network adapters can also be examined using:

Get-NetAdapter

and:

Get-NetIPConfiguration
DHCP

DHCP provides automatic network configuration to client computers.

The laboratory environment explored:

DHCP scopes
Address ranges
Default gateway
DNS server configuration
Lease allocation
Client DHCP configuration

A DHCP client configuration can be inspected using:

ipconfig /all

A client can request a new DHCP lease using:

ipconfig /release
ipconfig /renew
DNS

DNS converts hostnames into IP addresses and is particularly important in Windows Server and Active Directory environments.

DNS troubleshooting was performed using:

nslookup

and:

ipconfig /flushdns

Testing name resolution helps determine whether a problem is related to DNS rather than general network connectivity.

Network Connectivity Testing

Basic connectivity was tested using:

ping <IP-address>

and:

ping <hostname>

The difference between testing an IP address and testing a hostname can help identify DNS-related problems.

Other useful diagnostic commands include:

tracert <destination>
Test-NetConnection <destination>

For example:

Test-NetConnection 192.168.x.x -Port 53
